---
import CaseStudy from "~/layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import CaseHero from "~/components/case/CaseHero.astro";
import Closing from "~/components/case/Closing.astro";
import Lessons from "~/components/case/Lessons.astro";
import NextNav from "~/components/case/NextNav.astro";
import Scenes from "~/components/case/Scenes.astro";
import SceneStep from "~/components/case/SceneStep.astro";
import StepShot from "~/components/case/StepShot.astro";
import StepShots from "~/components/case/StepShots.astro";
import ExternalLink from "~/components/ui/ExternalLink.astro";
import Section from "~/components/ui/Section.astro";
import lobby from "~/assets/shots/r99-lobby.webp";
import combat from "~/assets/shots/r99-game-combat.webp";
import library from "~/assets/shots/tutor-library.webp";
import scryfall from "~/assets/shots/scryfall-home.webp";
import scryfallPhone from "~/assets/shots/scryfall-home-mobile.webp";

/* The one public repo of the suite; Rules of 99, Tutor and the search are private. */
const skills = "https://github.com/guuse/claude-mtg-skills";
---

<CaseStudy
  project="mtg-suite"
  title="MTG suite · Guus Ekkelenkamp"
  og="mtg-suite"
  description="Case study: the Magic: The Gathering tools I build for my friends, from a Commander table in the browser to a shared deck library."
  lightbox
>
  <CaseHero
    project="mtg-suite"
    meta="Personal · 2026 · one of the tools is open source:"
    title="MTG suite"
    line="Tools my friends and I use to learn Commander, keep our decks and play online when we can’t meet up."
  >
    <ExternalLink slot="meta" href={skills}>
      the deck-building skills
    </ExternalLink>
  </CaseHero>
  <Scenes id="story" title="How it works" gallery>
    <SceneStep scene="itch">
      <p>
        Magic: The Gathering is the card game my friends and I play most, usually Commander: four players,
        each with a 100-card deck built around one legendary creature, the commander. It’s a great game, but
        with over 33,000 cards there are a lot of exceptions to the rules. We wanted an easier way to learn
        it, and a way to play on the evenings we can’t sit at one table.
      </p>
      <p>
        My friends are my testers and they tell me straight when something’s wrong. I can fix it in the
        afternoon and we play with the fix that evening.
      </p>
    </SceneStep>
    <SceneStep scene="table">
      <h3>A table in the browser</h3>
      <p>
        Rules of 99 is Commander in the browser for two to four players, joined with a six-character room
        code. I didn’t write my own rules engine; that would take years. I run an open-source one headless on
        my server. My code sends each decision the engine needs to the right player’s browser and passes the
        answer back.
      </p>
      <p>
        In Magic a spell doesn’t happen right away. It goes on the stack, a pile where the last thing played
        resolves first, and every player gets priority, a chance to respond. At four players that’s a lot of
        questions, so I trimmed the stops and added auto-resolve for moments when you have nothing to play. It
        never skips a spell aimed at you or your cards.
      </p>
      <StepShot
        src={lobby}
        alt="Rules of 99 home screen after signing in"
        caption="Home after signing in: your decks and open tables."
      />
    </SceneStep>
    <SceneStep scene="hands">
      <h3>Hidden hands and replays</h3>
      <p>
        The server holds the whole game and sends each player only their own view. The others only see how
        many cards are in your hand, and libraries never leave the server. You can reload and sit back down,
        and finished games can be replayed turn by turn.
      </p>
      <p>
        Targeting had a bug. The engine numbers players from 0 and cards from 1, and the server looked up card
        ids first, so picking player 1 picked card 1 instead. Now every option says whether it’s a player or a
        card.
      </p>
      <StepShot
        src={combat}
        alt="Rules of 99 during combat: four attackers, with arrows to the player they attack"
        caption="Combat: four attackers, each with an arrow to its target."
      />
    </SceneStep>
    <SceneStep scene="decks">
      <h3>Tutor, our deck library</h3>
      <p>
        Tutor is our deck library and builder, and it enforces the deck rules. A card only fits a deck when
        its colours sit inside the commander’s, and banned cards stay out, so the search is exact. Each deck
        shows its curve and power bracket, so we can pick decks that suit each other.
      </p>
      <p>
        Friends can share their paper collections with the group, so before anyone buys a card you can see who
        already has one in a binder. When a deck is ready, one click takes it into Rules of 99, which can only
        read your decks.
      </p>
      <StepShot
        src={library}
        alt="Tutor deck library with commander art for each deck"
        caption="The deck library."
      />
    </SceneStep>
    <SceneStep scene="search">
      <h3>Finding cards in plain words</h3>
      <p>
        Scryfall is the card search most players use, and its syntax takes practice. In mine you describe the
        cards in normal words. A model writes the query and the server tests it against Scryfall first. You
        get a best match and two to four alternatives, each with a breakdown of what every part does, so you
        pick up the syntax as you go. For deck building there’s also a set of
        <ExternalLink href={skills}>open-source Claude Code skills</ExternalLink>.
      </p>
      <StepShots>
        <StepShot
          src={scryfall}
          alt="Scryfall, in plain words: a search box with a plain-language request and suggestion chips"
          caption="Describe the cards in normal words; the query is written and checked for you."
        />
        <StepShot
          src={scryfallPhone}
          alt="Scryfall, in plain words, on a phone"
          caption="On a phone."
          phone
        />
      </StepShots>
    </SceneStep>
    <SceneStep scene="friends">
      <h3>Bug reports and card updates</h3>
      <p>
        My friends can file a bug report straight from the table. One click on my triage page turns it into a
        GitHub issue, and closing the issue marks the report fixed. Every merge to main deploys to my homelab.
      </p>
      <p>
        A nightly job pulls in the engine’s latest card data, checks that the engine still starts on it and
        opens a pull request. The engine version itself is pinned; I only update it by hand.
      </p>
    </SceneStep>
  </Scenes>
  <Section id="lessons" title="Lessons">
    <Lessons
      items={[
        {
          title: "No more mock server",
          text: "A mock server once hid a bug that made the whole hand unclickable in real games, so I deleted it. Every test now plays against the real server.",
        },
        {
          title: "Removing another priority stop",
          text: "When I removed one more priority stop to speed games up, the scripted test players interacted less and one seat died early. Players got fewer chances to respond, so I put that stop back.",
        },
      ]}
    />
  </Section>
  <Closing stack="Built with: Java, React, TypeScript, WebSockets, Node and SQLite." />
  <NextNav
    prev={{ href: "/projects/homelab/", small: "Previous project", label: "Homelab", arrow: "left" }}
    next={{ href: "/projects/", small: "Back to", label: "all projects" }}
  />
</CaseStudy>
